---
name: "AI Code Review"
description: "Reviews the built change and reports problems"
tags: [check, review]
stage: check
---
Read the current Mohist issue details before interpreting this task:

```bash
mo issue view ${{ issue.number }} --project ${{ issue.projectId }}
```

Review the current change and write your findings to
`openspec/changes/issue-${{ issue.number }}/review.md`.

You are a reviewer, not a fixer: do not modify any file other than `review.md`.
A separate task disposes of every must-fix finding you report, so describe each
must-fix finding with enough context (what is wrong, where) for that task to
act on.

Review the changed files as they are now, against the issue's acceptance criteria
and the plan artifacts under `openspec/changes/issue-${{ issue.number }}/`. Judge only this change —
files under `openspec/changes/issue-${{ issue.number }}/` are this workflow's own artifacts, not
product deliverables.

## Verdict

Reach exactly one verdict and own it:

- PASS — no must-fix problems; the change is ready to merge.
- FAIL — at least one must-fix problem exists.

No middle outcome: do not FAIL to be safe, and do not PASS with reservations —
record reservations as observations instead.

A problem is must-fix only if leaving it unfixed makes the change wrong or
incomplete relative to the issue's acceptance criteria; cite the specific
criterion each must-fix finding violates. Everything else — possible
improvements, speculative risks, completeness beyond the issue's scope — goes
to an `Observations` section and does not affect the verdict.

## First review: full sweep

If you have not reviewed this change before, check every dimension below and
give each an explicit verdict ("checked, no issue" counts):

- Re-read the issue's acceptance criteria before the diff, so you judge against
  the issue rather than the change's own framing.
- Coverage: every acceptance criterion implemented.
- Correctness: the implementation actually satisfies each criterion.
- Consistency with the surrounding codebase and its conventions.
- Tests: the changed behavior is covered and verified.

Work adversarially within each dimension: try to construct the failure case
instead of reading for confirmation. Classify each finding as must-fix or
observation when you record it.

## Re-review: verify dispositions

If you reviewed this change before, verify instead of re-sweeping:

1. Check every finding from your previous review: fixed properly, or won't-fixed
   with a reason that holds. FAIL on any must-fix finding still unaddressed or
   whose won't-fix justification does not hold.
2. Check whether the fixes introduced regressions; a regression that meets the
   must-fix bar FAILs in any round.
3. A pre-existing problem missed in earlier rounds may still FAIL, but only if
   it meets the must-fix bar and you state why your earlier per-dimension
   verdicts did not catch it. If you cannot justify the miss, record it as an
   observation.

The final line of `review.md` MUST be exactly one of:
- `<promise>PASS</promise>` — no must-fix problems; the change is ready to merge.
- `<promise>FAIL</promise>` — must-fix problems found; list them first.
